# Rolling Telemetry Windows

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:ex_data_sketch, "~> 0.10.0"}
])
```

## Introduction

Rolling telemetry windows track metrics over time intervals using
probabilistic sketches. `ExDataSketch.Window` wraps any mergeable sketch
family in a fixed-size ring of tumbling slots and merges the live ones on
read -- no hand-rolled timer, no `GenServer` managing a list of retired
sketches. See `guides/windowing.md` for the full write-up of what "the last
N minutes" means for a tumbling window (not an exact sliding window) and
which sketch families can be windowed.

## Section 1: Basic Time-Windowed Sketch

```elixir
# 100ms slots, 2 of them retained -> a short window, for demo purposes.
# In production this would be something like every: :timer.minutes(1), keep: 5.
# Each batch below uses a disjoint user-ID range so the printed estimate is
# unambiguous; sleeping 300ms (three slot durations) between batches, with
# keep: 2, reliably ages the previous batch's slot out before the next one.
window = ExDataSketch.Window.new(:hll, [p: 14], every: 100, keep: 2)

# Batch 1: 200 distinct users
window = Enum.reduce(1..200, window, fn i, w -> ExDataSketch.Window.update(w, "batch1_user_#{i}") end)
IO.puts("After batch 1: #{Float.round(ExDataSketch.Window.estimate(window), 0)} (true: 200)")

# Wait long enough (three slot durations) for batch 1's slot to age out
Process.sleep(300)

# Batch 2: 500 distinct (disjoint) users, in a new slot
window = Enum.reduce(1..500, window, fn i, w -> ExDataSketch.Window.update(w, "batch2_user_#{i}") end)
IO.puts("After batch 2: #{Float.round(ExDataSketch.Window.estimate(window), 0)} (true: ~500 -- batch 1 has aged out)")

# Wait again for batch 2's slot to age out
Process.sleep(300)

# Batch 3: only 50 distinct (disjoint) users -- demonstrates window aging
window = Enum.reduce(1..50, window, fn i, w -> ExDataSketch.Window.update(w, "batch3_user_#{i}") end)
IO.puts("After batch 3: #{Float.round(ExDataSketch.Window.estimate(window), 0)} (true: ~50 -- batch 2 has aged out)")

IO.inspect(ExDataSketch.Window.slots(window) |> Enum.map(fn {key, _sketch} -> key end), label: "live slot keys")
```

## Section 2: Deterministic Testing (No Sleeping)

The demo above uses the real clock, appropriate for exploring the module
interactively. Application tests should not sleep -- `update/3` and `tick/2`
accept an explicit `now` in milliseconds, and `new/3` accepts a `:time_fn`
for a fully controlled clock:

```elixir
window = ExDataSketch.Window.new(:hll, [p: 14], every: 1000, keep: 3, time_fn: fn -> 3000 end)

window = window |> ExDataSketch.Window.update("a", 0) |> ExDataSketch.Window.update("b", 3000)

# slot 0 has already aged out of the 3-slot window as of "now" = 3000
IO.inspect(ExDataSketch.Window.slots(window) |> Enum.map(fn {key, _sketch} -> key end), label: "live slot keys")
```

## Section 3: Persistence for Long-Term Storage

`Window.serialize/1` and `deserialize/1` persist a window's configuration
and live slot contents (each slot through its own sketch module's own
`serialize/1`, so sketch state stays in the standard EXSK v2 format). Persist
the resulting binary to any `ExDataSketch.Storage` backend the same way you
would a plain sketch:

```elixir
table = :ets.new(:sketch_windows, [:set, :public, :named_table])

window = ExDataSketch.Window.new(:hll, [p: 14], every: 100, keep: 5)
window = Enum.reduce(1..500, window, fn i, w -> ExDataSketch.Window.update(w, "user_#{i}") end)

binary = ExDataSketch.Window.serialize(window)
:ets.insert(table, {"window:2024-01-15:00", binary})

[{_key, loaded_binary}] = :ets.lookup(table, "window:2024-01-15:00")
{:ok, restored} = ExDataSketch.Window.deserialize(loaded_binary)
IO.puts("Restored window has #{map_size(restored.slots)} live slot(s) (module: #{inspect(restored.module)})")

:ets.delete(table)
```

## Section 4: Telemetry Integration

`Window` emits `[:ex_data_sketch, :window, :roll]` whenever a slot ages out
of an `update/2,3`, `update_many/2`, or `tick/2` call (not from `estimate/1`,
`merged/1`, or `slots/1`, which read transiently without mutating state):

```elixir
:telemetry.attach("demo-window-handler", [:ex_data_sketch, :window, :roll], fn _name, measurements, metadata, _config ->
  IO.puts("Rolled #{metadata.sketch_type} window: #{measurements.dropped_count} slot(s) dropped, #{measurements.slot_count} still live")
end, nil)

window = ExDataSketch.Window.new(:hll, [p: 14], every: 100, keep: 1)
window = ExDataSketch.Window.update(window, "a", 0)
ExDataSketch.Window.update(window, "b", 500)

:telemetry.detach("demo-window-handler")
```

## Section 5: Operational Guidance

**Window sizing** (`every:` slot duration, `keep:` slot count):

- `every: :timer.minutes(1), keep: 5` -- 5 minutes of history (good for dashboards)
- `every: :timer.minutes(5), keep: 12` -- 1 hour (good for alerting)
- `every: :timer.hours(1), keep: 24` -- 1 day (good for reporting)

**Memory**: each live slot is a full sketch. At `p: 14`, each HLL slot is
about 16KB; a 5-slot window is about 80KB (plus one extra slot's worth while
the oldest is still live alongside a freshly started current slot).

**Accuracy**: tumbling windows retain between `keep - 1` and `keep` full
slots of history, not exactly `keep` -- see `guides/windowing.md` for why.
Narrower slots (`every: :timer.seconds(1), keep: 300` instead of
`every: :timer.minutes(1), keep: 5`) trade more live sketches for tighter
boundary accuracy.

**Which families can be windowed**: any family with `merge/2`. `Cuckoo`,
`XorFilter`, and `FilterChain` are rejected at `Window.new/3` with a clear
error. `estimate/1` additionally requires the family to support
`ExDataSketch.estimate/1` (cardinality, quantile, and heavy-hitter families);
`CMS` and the mergeable membership filters use `merged/1` instead.
